## 🧩 3. Essential Graph Patterns (With Architectural Flowcharts)

### Pattern 1: Prompt Chaining (Linear Flow)
The simplest model. Executing LLM calls sequentially where later calls rely on the refined outputs of earlier calls.
```mermaid
graph LR
    A[Raw Input] --> B[LLM 1: Draft Text]
    B --> C[LLM 2: Analyze Tone]
    C --> D[LLM 3: Rewrite formally]
    D --> E[Final Output]
```

### Pattern 2: Dynamic Routing (The Orchestrator)
A decision node statically forces downstream execution conditionally based on state variables or an LLM classification.
```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Input Ticket] --> B{Router LLM Node: Classify Intent}
    B -->|Tech Support| C[Code Expert Agent Node]
    B -->|Finance| D[Billing API Agent Node]
    B -->|General QA| E[Basic RAG Node]
```

### Pattern 3: Parallel Spawning & Aggregation
When speed and broad context is required, the Graph spawns independent tasks simultaneously and merges them.
```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Controversial Topic Prompt] --> B(Search NYT Node)
    A --> C(Search Fox News Node)
    A --> D(Search Twitter Node)
    B --> E[Aggregator Node: Synthesize Neutral Report]
    C --> E
    D --> E
```

### Pattern 4: The Retry / Reflection Loop (Critical)
Using an Evaluator Node (or guardrail) to conditionally bounce the execution back to the Generator Node until a quality threshold is organically met.
```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Start] --> B[Generator Node: Write Email]
    B --> C{Evaluator Node: Score Tone}
    C -->|Score < 8/10| B
    C -->|Score >= 8/10| D[End: Send Email]
```

